Given, \[y(t)=\frac{d}{dt}\left\{e^{-t}x(t)\right\}\] <strong>(i) Linearity:</strong><br/>For input \(x_1(t)\), \[y_1(t)=\frac{d}{dt}\left\{e^{-t}x_1(t)\right\}\] Multiplying by scalar \(a\), \[a\,y_1(t)
=
a\frac{d}{dt}\left\{e^{-t}x_1(t)\right\}
\tag{i}\] For input \(x_2(t)\), \[y_2(t)=\frac{d}{dt}\left\{e^{-t}x_2(t)\right\}\] Multiplying by scalar \(b\), \[b\,y_2(t)
=
b\frac{d}{dt}\left\{e^{-t}x_2(t)\right\}
\tag{ii}\] Adding (i) and (ii), \[a\,y_1(t)+b\,y_2(t)
=
a\frac{d}{dt}\left\{e^{-t}x_1(t)\right\}
+
b\frac{d}{dt}\left\{e^{-t}x_2(t)\right\}
\tag{iii}\] Now let the input be \[a\,x_1(t)+b\,x_2(t)\] with output \(y_3(t)\). \[y_3(t)
=
\frac{d}{dt}
\left\{
e^{-t}\bigl(a\,x_1(t)+b\,x_2(t)\bigr)
\right\}\] \[y_3(t)
=
a\frac{d}{dt}\left\{e^{-t}x_1(t)\right\}
+
b\frac{d}{dt}\left\{e^{-t}x_2(t)\right\}
\tag{iv}\] From (iii) and (iv), \[y_3(t)=a\,y_1(t)+b\,y_2(t)\] Therefore, the system satisfies superposition and is <strong>linear</strong>. <strong>(ii) Time Variance:</strong><br/>For delayed input \(x(t-t_0)\), \[y_1(t)
=
\frac{d}{dt}
\left\{
e^{-t}x(t-t_0)
\right\}
\tag{v}\] The delayed version of the output is \[y(t-t_0)
=
\frac{d}{dt}
\left\{
e^{-(t-t_0)}x(t-t_0)
\right\}
\tag{vi}\] From (v) and (vi), \[y_1(t)\neq y(t-t_0)\] Hence, the system is <strong>time-varying</strong>. <strong>(iii) Stability:</strong><br/>For bounded input \[x(t)=u(t),\] \[y(t)
=
\frac{d}{dt}
\left\{
e^{-t}u(t)
\right\}\] \[y(t)
=
e^{-t}\delta(t)+u(t)(-e^{-t})\] For bounded input, the output is unbounded due to the impulse term. Therefore, the system is <strong>unstable</strong>. <strong>(iv) Memory:</strong><br/>A system is memoryless if the output depends only on the present input. Differentiation depends on neighboring values of the input signal. Hence, the system possesses <strong>memory</strong> and is a dynamic system. (D) The system has memory

Given \(y(n)-\dfrac{1}{3}y(n-1)=x(n)\), \(x(n)=\left(\dfrac{1}{2}\right)^{n}u(n)\). Taking the Z-transform under initial-rest conditions, \[Y(z)-\frac{1}{3}z^{-1}Y(z)=\frac{z}{z-\tfrac{1}{2}}
\ \Rightarrow\
Y(z)=\frac{z}{\left(z-\tfrac{1}{2}\right)\left(1-\tfrac{1}{3}z^{-1}\right)}
=\frac{z^{2}}{\left(z-\tfrac{1}{2}\right)\left(z-\tfrac{1}{3}\right)}.\] Expanding \(Y(z)/z\) in partial fractions, \[\frac{Y(z)}{z}=\frac{3}{z-\tfrac12}-\frac{2}{z-\tfrac13}
\ \Rightarrow\
Y(z)=\frac{3z}{z-\tfrac12}-\frac{2z}{z-\tfrac13}.\] Taking the inverse Z-transform, \[y(n)=-2\left(\frac{1}{3}\right)^{n}+3\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{n},\quad n\ge 0.\] B) \(-2\left(\dfrac{1}{3}\right)^{n}+3\left(\dfrac{1}{2}\right)^{n}\)

